.TH CHMAP_DECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_declare \- declare a hash map handle and its companion type variables
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_declare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro. The macro creates nothing: the handle holds no map until one of
the init macros runs.

This is the declare half of a declare-then-create pair, and
chmap_init(3), chmap_init_mp(3), chmap_init_ch(3) and chmap_init_full(3)
are the matching create half. chmap_construct(3) and its variants do
both in one step and are the shorter way to write the common case. Two
separate steps are what a handle needs when the code declares it in one
place and creates it in another, for example a map whose allocator a
condition chooses, or a map that the code creates only after the
enclosing function checks its arguments.

The macro leaves the handle and the companion type variables
uninitialized, so the handle holds no usable value, not even NULL,
before an init macro assigns one. chmap_declare_scoped(3) sets the
handle to NULL instead, and also destroys the map at scope exit.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and, with
         val_t, which implementation strategy the init macro selects
val_t    Value type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Expands to a sequence of declarations, so put it where a declaration is
allowed; it takes a trailing semicolon of its own
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a
function that receives the handle needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
Nothing is destroyed automatically; call chmap_destroy(3) to destroy the
map that an init macro creates into this handle
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Every other type-inferred macro reads the handle, so do not call one
between this declaration and an init macro: such a call reads an
uninitialized variable.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_declare(scores, char *, int);
if (use_custom_allocator) {
    chmap_init_mp(scores, &my_procs);
} else {
    chmap_init(scores);
}
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_mp (3),
.BR chmap_init_ch (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_redeclare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
